  "text": "To find the least possible total number of meals they need to prepare, we need to find a common multiple of the number of meals per group that ensures the same ratio of chicken sandwiches, vegetarian sandwiches, and salads for all groups.\n\nAthletes:\n- Chicken sandwiches: 3 per box\n- Vegetarian sandwiches: 2 per box\n- Salads: 3 per box\n\nCoaches:\n- Chicken sandwiches: 2 per box\n- Vegetarian sandwiches: 1 per box\n- Salads: 2 per box\n\nOfficials:\n- Chicken sandwiches: 1 per package\n- Vegetarian sandwiches: 1 per package\n- Salads: 1 per package\n\nNow, let's find the least common multiple (LCM) of the number of meals per box or package for each group:\n\nFor Athletes (Chicken sandwiches: 3, Vegetarian sandwiches: 2, Salads: 3):\n- LCM(3, 2, 3) = 6\n\nFor Coaches (Chicken sandwiches: 2, Vegetarian sandwiches: 1, Salads: 2):\n- LCM(2, 1, 2) = 2\n\nFor Officials (Chicken sandwiches: 1, Vegetarian sandwiches: 1, Salads: 1):\n- LCM(1, 1, 1) = 1\n\nNow, we need to multiply the LCMs of each group to find the least possible total number of meals:\n\nTotal meals = LCM(Athletes) + LCM(Coaches) + LCM(Officials)\nTotal meals = 6 + 2 + 1 = 9\n\nTherefore, the least possible total number of meals they need to prepare is \\boxed{9}.",
